    /* ══════════ SCIENCE — deeper batch ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_genetics", patterns: ["what is genetics","heredity","what is dna","genes and chromosomes","dominant recessive traits","gregor mendel","why do i look like my parents","genetics kya hai","what is a gene","inherited traits examples"],
        responses: ["🧬 **Genetics — why you're a remix of your family:**\n- **DNA** = the instruction manual in (almost) every cell — a twisted ladder (double helix) written in 4 chemical letters: A, T, G, C. Your full manual = ~3 billion letters!\n- **Gene** = one instruction (e.g., 'eye colour: brown') · **Chromosome** = a packed chapter — humans have **46 (23 pairs)**: half from mom, half from dad\n- **Heredity** = traits passing down. **Dominant** genes shout (brown eyes), **recessive** whisper (blue eyes — needs BOTH copies)\n- **Gregor Mendel** 🌱 — the monk who grew 28,000 pea plants and discovered the rules (1860s) — 'Father of Genetics'\n- Fun: your DNA is 99.9% identical to every human — and ~60% shared with a BANANA 🍌. Unique AND connected!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_cell_division", patterns: ["cell division","mitosis","meiosis","how do cells multiply","difference between mitosis and meiosis","cell cycle","why do cells divide","koshika vibhajan","how does a cut heal","growth and cell division"],
        responses: ["🔬 **Cell division — how 1 cell became YOU (30+ trillion of them):**\n\n**Mitosis (body cells):** 1 cell → 2 IDENTICAL copies\n1. Chromosomes duplicate · 2. line up in the middle · 3. pull apart to opposite ends · 4. cell pinches into two\n→ Used for **growth & repair** — that's how a cut heals and how you got taller 📏\n\n**Meiosis (sex cells only):** 1 cell → 4 cells with HALF the chromosomes (23 each)\n→ Makes sperm & egg cells; when they combine: 23+23 = 46 — a brand-new unique human! It even SHUFFLES genes (crossing over), so siblings differ (except identical twins)\n\nOne fertilized egg → ~200+ rounds of division → you. Cell division = life's copy-paste, with an occasional beautiful typo called evolution 🧬"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_adolescence", patterns: ["adolescence","puberty","changes during puberty","what is puberty class 8","reaching age","voice change in boys","menstruation basics","hormones in adolescence","height growth spurt","kishoravastha"],
        responses: ["🌱 **Adolescence ('Reaching Age', Class 8 science — the honest, scientific version):**\n- **Adolescence** = the bridge from child to adult, roughly **10–19 years**. Your body runs a hormone-driven upgrade 📶\n- **Changes in everyone:** growth spurt (height!), body shape changes, oilier skin (hello, pimples), more sweating, mood swings, brain rewiring (better reasoning, bigger feelings)\n- **Boys:** voice deepens (voice box grows → 'Adam's apple'), facial/body hair, shoulders broaden\n- **Girls:** hips widen, breasts develop, **menstruation** begins — a healthy monthly cycle where the uterus lining sheds (blood + tissue) if there's no pregnancy. It's biology, not embarrassment — it means the body works correctly ✅\n- **Hormones** = chemical messengers: testosterone (boys), estrogen (girls), growth hormone (everyone)\n- **Survival tips:** daily bath, iron-rich food during periods, sleep 8-9h (growth happens while sleeping!), talk to parents/teachers/doctors with questions — that's what they're for\n\nEveryone goes through it at their own pace. Early, late, or in-between = all normal 🌈"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_stars", patterns: ["what is a star","how do stars shine","life cycle of a star","what is a galaxy","milky way galaxy","what is a light year","types of stars","supernova","neutron star","tare kya hote hain","why do stars twinkle"],
        responses: ["⭐ **Stars — the universe's fusion reactors:**\n- A star = a giant ball of gas (mostly hydrogen) crushing itself under gravity until the core ignites **nuclear fusion**: hydrogen → helium + ENORMOUS light/heat. Our Sun fuses ~600 million tonnes of hydrogen PER SECOND ☀️\n- **Life cycle:** nebula (gas cloud) → protostar → main-sequence star (like the Sun, ~10 billion years) → red giant → **white dwarf** (Sun's fate) — but massive stars go dramatic: **supernova** explosion 💥 → **neutron star** (a sugar-cube of it weighs 1 billion tonnes!) or **black hole**\n- **Galaxy** = a city of stars — ours, the **Milky Way**, holds 100–400 BILLION stars and you're seeing its edge as that milky band at night 🌌\n- **Light year** = DISTANCE light travels in a year (~9.46 trillion km). The nearest star (Proxima Centauri) is 4.2 light years away — its light left before your great-grandparents were born\n- **Twinkling** = Earth's turbulent air bending starlight. Astronauts see steady stars!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_universe", patterns: ["the universe","big bang theory","how did the universe begin","what is a black hole","age of the universe","universe kitna bada hai","cosmos","is the universe expanding","event horizon","singularity"],
        responses: ["🌌 **The Universe — everything, everywhere:**\n- **Age:** ~**13.8 billion years**, begun (as far as we know) in the **Big Bang** — not an explosion IN space, but space itself expanding from an ultra-hot dense state. Evidence: galaxies flying apart (redshift) + leftover 'baby universe' radiation everywhere (CMB)\n- **Scale:** ~2 trillion galaxies · our Milky Way is one of them · Earth is speck #3 around an average star 😵\n- **Black holes** = gravity so strong that inside the **event horizon**, nothing escapes — not even light. Made from collapsed giant stars (and monster ones sit in galaxy centers — Sagittarius A* in ours = 4 million suns!). We PHOTOGRAPHED one in 2019 (M87*) 📸\n- The universe is still **expanding** — and accelerating (blame 'dark energy', which with 'dark matter' makes up ~95% of everything… that we can't see. Astronomers stay humble 🙃)\n- Time near a black hole slows down — yes, like Interstellar. Physics is wilder than movies!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_mirrors", patterns: ["reflection of light","types of mirrors","concave convex mirror","plane mirror image","mirror image laterally inverted","where are concave mirrors used","darpan ke prakar","why is ambulance written reversed","mirror formula basics"],
        responses: ["🪞 **Mirrors & reflection — light's boomerang:**\n\n**Laws of reflection:** (1) angle of incidence = angle of reflection (2) incoming ray, reflected ray and the normal all lie in one plane. Simple, non-negotiable ✅\n\n**Three mirror types:**\n- **Plane** (flat, your bathroom mirror) — image: same size, upright, **laterally inverted** (left↔right swap — that's why AMBULANCE is written reversed, so drivers see it correctly in THEIR mirror! 🚑)\n- **Concave** (caved in) — the magic one: close-up = big upright image (shaving/makeup/dentist mirrors), far = real inverted image (torch & headlight reflectors, telescope mirrors, solar furnaces)\n- **Convex** (bulging out) — always small, upright, WIDE view → vehicle side-mirrors ('objects in mirror are closer than they appear!' 🚗), shop security mirrors\n\nFun: periscopes use two plane mirrors at 45°; kaleidoscopes use three. Reflection = why you exist in mirrors at all — photons bouncing off you, then the glass, then your eye ✨"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_lenses", patterns: ["refraction of light","lenses concave convex","why does a pencil look bent in water","myopia hypermetropia","nearsightedness farsightedness","how do spectacles work","lens used in magnifying glass","refraction kya hai","prism splits light","power of a lens"],
        responses: ["🔍 **Lenses & refraction — light bending to your will:**\n- **Refraction** = light CHANGING direction when it enters a new medium (air→water→glass) because its speed changes. That's why a pencil looks bent in a glass of water, and pools look shallower than they are 🏊\n- **Convex lens** (thick middle, 'magnifying') — converges light → magnifying glasses, cameras, eyes!, microscopes, telescopes\n- **Concave lens** (thin middle) — diverges light → peepholes, spectacle corrections\n- **Your EYE has a convex lens** — it focuses light on the retina (the screen at the back)\n\n**Vision defects & fixes:**\n- **Myopia (near-sighted):** see near fine, far blurry → image forms BEFORE retina → fixed with **concave** spectacles 👓\n- **Hypermetropia (far-sighted):** near blurry → image would form BEHIND retina → fixed with **convex** lenses\n- **Cataract** = cloudy lens → replaced surgically (one of medicine's greatest fixes!)\n\nBonus: a **prism** refracts each colour by a different amount → splits white light into VIBGYOR 🌈. Newton's favourite party trick."] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_pressure", patterns: ["buoyancy","archimedes principle","why do ships float","why does a needle sink but ship float","upthrust","pressure in liquids","atmospheric pressure","why are bag straps wide","pressure force area"],
        responses: ["⚓ **Pressure & buoyancy — why steel ships float:**\n- **Pressure = Force ÷ Area** (Pascal, Pa). Same force, smaller area = MORE pressure — that's why knives are sharp, nails pointy, and school-bag straps WIDE (big area = gentle on shoulders 🎒)\n- **Liquids push too:** pressure grows with depth (dam walls are thicker at the bottom!) and pushes equally in all directions\n- **Atmospheric pressure:** the entire sky sits on you — ~101 kPa at sea level! You don't flatten because your body pushes back equally. Drinking with a straw = you remove air, atmosphere PUSHES the drink up 🥤\n- **Buoyancy/Upthrust:** water pushes UP on anything in it. **Archimedes' principle** (shouted as 'Eureka!' from a bathtub 🛁): upthrust = weight of the liquid DISPLACED\n- **The ship paradox:** a steel needle sinks (displaces tiny water = tiny upthrust). A steel SHIP is hollow — its bowl shape displaces TONNES of water → huge upthrust → floats! Submarines float/sink on demand by flooding or emptying ballast tanks 🌊\n- Ice floats (less dense than water), hot-air balloons rise (less dense than air) — same density story everywhere!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_static", patterns: ["static electricity","lightning","why do clothes stick in winter","electric charge","earthing","lightning conductor","what causes lightning","spark when removing sweater","positive negative charges","static bijli"],
        responses: ["⚡ **Static electricity & lightning — sparks of imbalance:**\n- Everything is made of atoms with + (protons) and − (electrons) charges. Rub two things together and electrons JUMP ship → one object becomes negative, the other positive → **static charge**\n- Opposites attract: that's the sweater-crackle in winter, balloons sticking to walls, comb-picking-up-paper tricks, and clothes clinging in the dryer 👕\n- **Lightning** = static on a MONSTER scale: storm clouds grind ice particles until the cloud bottom goes hugely negative → the ground below goes positive → when the gap can't hold, the air itself breaks down and ZAP — a giant spark at ~30,000°C (5× hotter than the Sun's surface!) 🌩️ Thunder = the sonic boom of that superheated air\n- **Earthing** = giving excess charge a path to the ground (that third pin in your plug!) · **Lightning conductors** = metal rods on buildings that politely escort the bolt to earth\n- Safety: inside a building/car during storms = safe; under a tree = the WORST idea. Count flash→boom seconds ÷3 = storm distance in km ⏱️"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_work_power", patterns: ["work in physics","what is power in physics","joule and watt","formula for work","work energy power class 9","unit of power","when is work done","work force displacement","1 watt means","power ka formula"],
        responses: ["🏋️ **Work, Energy & Power — physics' accountancy:**\n- **WORK** is done ONLY when a force MOVES something: **W = F × d** (force × displacement). Pushing a wall for an hour? Zero work (physics is heartless 😄). Unit: **joule (J)** = 1 newton × 1 metre\n- **ENERGY** = the capacity to do work (also joules). Kinetic = ½mv² · Potential (gravity) = mgh\n- **POWER** = how FAST work happens: **P = W ÷ t**. Unit: **watt (W)** = 1 joule per second. Two climbers reach the same roof — same work — but the sprinter used more POWER 🏃\n- Scale feel: you climbing stairs ≈ 200-400 W · old bulb = 60 W · LED bulb = 9 W (same light, ⅙ the power!) · microwave ≈ 1000 W · your 'electricity bill unit' = **1 kWh** = 3.6 million joules\n- 1 horsepower ≈ 746 W (James Watt needed a way to sell steam engines against horses 🐴)\n\nWork-energy theorem: net work done = change in kinetic energy. Physics in one line!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_carbon", patterns: ["carbon compounds","organic chemistry","what is a hydrocarbon","why is carbon special","covalent bonds","diamond and graphite","saturated unsaturated compounds","methane ethane","carbon ke yauugik","what are alkanes alkenes"],
        responses: ["💎 **Carbon — the LEGO atom of life:**\n- Why carbon rules: it has **4 outer electrons** → forms 4 strong **covalent bonds** (sharing electrons) with other atoms AND with itself → chains, rings, branches — millions of compounds (more than all other elements combined!)\n- **Hydrocarbons** = carbon + hydrogen only:\n  · **Alkanes** (single bonds, 'saturated'): methane CH₄ (cooking gas!), ethane, propane, butane (LPG) 🔥\n  · **Alkenes** (a double bond, 'unsaturated'): ethene — ripens fruits artificially!\n  · **Alkynes** (triple bond): ethyne = welding fuel\n- Same atoms, different arrangement = **allotropes**: **diamond** (tetrahedral network — hardest natural) vs **graphite** (flat slippery sheets — pencil 'lead' & lubricant) — both pure carbon! ✏️💎\n- Fuels (petrol, CNG) = hydrocarbons; burning them = C+H meeting O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + energy\n- Everything alive — sugar, DNA, proteins, YOU — is carbon-based. Silicon dreams, carbon does 🧪"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_plastics", patterns: ["plastics and polymers","what is a polymer","thermoplastics thermosetting","why is plastic bad for environment","types of plastic","polythene","biodegradable plastic","plastic recycling codes","plastic kyun nuksandeh hai","bakelite melamine"],
        responses: ["🥤 **Plastics & polymers — long molecular chains:**\n- **Polymer** = 'many parts' — a giant molecule of repeating units (monomers) linked like a chain. Natural ones exist: cotton (cellulose), wool (protein), rubber!\n- **Synthetic plastics:** polythene (bags), PVC (pipes), PET (bottles — check the ♳ triangle codes!), nylon (fibres), teflon (non-stick)\n- **Thermoplastics** 🔁 — soften on heating, reshape forever (PET, polythene) → RECYCLABLE\n- **Thermosetting** 🔥 — set once, then refuse to melt (bakelite — switches/handles, melamine — unbreakable crockery) → hard to recycle\n- **The problem:** plastic lasts centuries, breaks into **microplastics** (found in oceans, food, even human blood 😬), chokes drains & animals; burning it releases poisons\n- **The fixes:** say no to single-use, carry cloth bags & steel bottles, segregate waste for recycling, and support bioplastics (made from corn/starch — they compost!)\n- Plastic isn't evil — syringes, helmets, water pipes save lives. WASTING it is the evil 🌍"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_combustion", patterns: ["combustion","types of combustion","what is fuel","calorific value","flammable inflammable","how does fire work","fire triangle","how to extinguish fire","aag kaise bujhaye","why is cng better than petrol"],
        responses: ["🔥 **Combustion — the chemistry of fire:**\n- **Combustion** = a substance reacting with oxygen, releasing heat & light. Fuel + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + energy\n- **The FIRE TRIANGLE** (all 3 needed): 🔥 fuel + oxygen + heat (ignition temperature). Remove ANY corner → fire dies — that's how firefighting works: water removes heat, a blanket/CO₂ removes oxygen, shutting the gas removes fuel 🧯\n- **Types:** rapid (a candle, burning paper), spontaneous (oily rags self-igniting!), explosion (fireworks, crackers — sudden gas + heat + sound) 💥\n- **Ignition temperature** = the minimum heat a fuel needs to catch fire. **Inflammable** = catches fire EASILY (petrol, LPG, alcohol — confusing word, it does NOT mean 'doesn't burn'!)\n- **Calorific value** = energy per kg (kJ/kg): hydrogen tops (~150,000), CNG beats petrol, wood is low + smoky\n- **Better fuels:** CNG/LPG burn cleaner (less CO & soot); petrol/diesel engines still poison city air\n- **Danger:** incomplete combustion in closed rooms (gensets, coal heaters) makes **carbon monoxide** — the silent killer. Ventilation saves lives 🌬️"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_biodiversity", patterns: ["biodiversity","endangered species","red data book","extinct animals","why protect biodiversity","endemic species","conservation of wildlife","biodiversity kya hai","species extinction","dodo dinosaur extinct"],
        responses: ["🐅 **Biodiversity — Earth's family album:**\n- **Biodiversity** = the variety of ALL life — species, genes, ecosystems. India is one of the world's 17 **'megadiverse'** countries (8% of all recorded species!) 🇮🇳\n- **Endangered** = at risk of extinction: Bengal tiger, one-horned rhino, Asiatic lion (Gir only!), snow leopard, Great Indian Bustard (<150 left 😢), Ganges dolphin\n- **Extinct** = gone forever: dinosaurs, dodo, passenger pigeon, cheetah (from India — now REINTRODUCED from Africa in 2022! 🐆)\n- **Endemic** = found ONLY in one place: lion-tailed macaque (Western Ghats), Kashmir stag\n- **Red Data Book** = the official record of threatened species · **Red List** by IUCN\n- **Why care?** Every species is a thread: bees pollinate ⅓ of our food, forests make rain & oxygen, coral reefs feed oceans, and medicines hide in plants (25% of modern drugs came from plants!)\n- **Protection:** national parks & sanctuaries (Project Tiger 1973 — tigers went 1,827→3,167 📈), biosphere reserves, no-poaching laws, seed banks, and YOU: don't buy wildlife products, plant native trees, spread the word 🌳"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "sci_instruments", patterns: ["scientific instruments","what does a microscope do","telescope types","instruments and their uses","stethoscope barometer thermometer","what is a seismograph","laboratory instruments","scientific devices names","upkaran aur unke upyog","what machine measures humidity"],
        responses: ["🔬 **Scientific instruments — the senses of science:**\n- **Microscope** — makes tiny things big (cells, bacteria); electron microscopes zoom to ATOM scale (2 million×!)\n- **Telescope** — grabs distant light: refracting (lenses, Galileo's), reflecting (mirrors, huge modern ones), and space telescopes like Hubble & James Webb (infrared eyes on the early universe 🔭)\n- **Stethoscope** — amplifies heart/lung sounds 🫀\n- **Thermometer** — temperature · **Barometer** — air pressure (falling = storm coming!) · **Hygrometer** — humidity · **Anemometer** — wind speed · **Rain gauge** — rainfall\n- **Seismograph** — records earthquake waves (Richter scale!)\n- **ECG/EEG** — heart & brain electrical tracings · **X-ray/MRI** — seeing inside bodies\n- **Balance/spring scale** — mass & weight · **Ammeter/voltmeter** — current & voltage · **Multimeter** — both in one\n- **Spectroscope** — splits light to reveal what stars are MADE of (that's how we know the Sun has helium — discovered in the Sun before Earth!)\n- Every instrument = humanity wearing glasses it couldn't before 👓"] });

    /* ══════════ SST — HISTORY deeper ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_south_india", patterns: ["chola dynasty","south indian dynasties","pandya chera kings","vijayanagara empire","pallava dynasty","rajaraja chola","brihadeeswara temple","krishnadevaraya","chola navy","south india history","tanjavur big temple"],
        responses: ["🛕 **South India's golden dynasties:**\n- **Cheras, Cholas, Pandyas** — the ancient Tamil trio (mentioned even by Ashoka & Greek traders!). The Pandyas sent ambassadors to Rome 🏛️\n- **Chola Empire (9th–13th c.)** — India's greatest sea power: **Rajaraja Chola I** built the **Brihadeeswara Temple** at Thanjavur (1010 CE — the vimana's 80-tonne capstone, no crane, still standing!) · his son Rajendra sailed to Southeast Asia, and Chola influence reached Sri Lanka, Maldives, Indonesia! Bronze Nataraja statues from this era are world-famous 🕺\n- **Pallavas** (Kanchipuram) — carved the shore temples of **Mahabalipuram** (UNESCO!)\n- **Vijayanagara Empire (1336–1646)** — Hampi's bazaar city was richer than Rome per travellers; **Krishnadevaraya** (Tenali Raman's king!) presided over the golden age\n- Later: Marathas of Thanjavur, the Nawabs, and Tipu Sultan of Mysore (rockets vs the British! 🚀)\n\nSouth India gave the world: temple mathematics, the 'zero-dotted' Bakhshali style trade, Carnatic music, and the decimal astronomy of Kerala school (Madhava did calculus bits 250 years before Newton-Leibniz!) 🤯"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_maratha", patterns: ["shivaji maharaj","maratha empire","chhatrapati shivaji","maratha guerrilla warfare","shivaji forts","peshwa bajirao","maratha history","shiva ji ki kahani","third battle of panipat maratha","ashtapradhan"],
        responses: ["⚔️ **Shivaji Maharaj & the Maratha Empire:**\n- **Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1630–1680)** — crowned in 1674 at Raigad, built an empire on **guerrilla warfare** ('ganimi kava'): speed, surprise, mountain forts (Pratapgad, Sinhagad, Raigad — 300+ forts!), and a navy (Kanhoji Angre later ruled the Konkan seas 🚢)\n- Legend: the Afzal Khan encounter at Pratapgad (1659) — 'wagh nakh' (tiger claws) answered a treacherous embrace. Escape from Agra in sweet baskets? Peak Shivaji 🧠\n- **His statecraft:** Ashta Pradhan (council of 8 ministers), fair taxes, respect for women & mosques, promotion of Marathi/Sanskrit over court-Persian\n- **After him:** the Peshwas (Balaji Vishwanath → **Bajirao I**, who never lost a battle!) expanded Maratha power across India — from Attock to Cuttack\n- **1761: Third Battle of Panipat** vs Ahmad Shah Abdali — a catastrophic loss that emptied the treasury of a generation of leaders (and changed 1760s Indian history forever)\n- The Marathas remained kingmakers until 1818. Shivaji's Hindavi Swarajya = the idea of self-rule, 200 years before 1947 🇮🇳"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_sikh", patterns: ["sikh empire","maharaja ranjit singh","ten gurus of sikhism","guru nanak dev","guru gobind singh","khalsa","golden temple history","sikh history","sher e punjab","guru granth sahib"],
        responses: ["🦁 **The Sikh Empire & the Gurus:**\n- **Guru Nanak Dev** (1469–1539) founded Sikhism — one God, honest work, sharing (langar: the world's biggest free kitchens 🍲), rejecting caste & empty ritual\n- **Ten Gurus** carried the light; **Guru Gobind Singh** created the **Khalsa** (1699) — the initiated brotherhood (Singhs & Kaurs, the 5 Ks) — and declared the scripture, **Guru Granth Sahib**, the eternal Guru (a book as living guide — unique in world religion 📖)\n- **Maharaja Ranjit Singh (1780–1839), 'Sher-e-Punjab'** — unified the misls into the **Sikh Empire** from Lahore: religiously plural court (Muslim, Hindu, Sikh ministers), a modernized army (even French generals!), and he gilded the Harmandir Sahib → the **Golden Temple** ✨\n- His empire held the Khyber Pass — the ONLY Indian power the British couldn't conquer while he lived. After his death, two Anglo-Sikh wars (1845-49) — the British called Sikh troops the bravest they faced — and Punjab was annexed (the young Maharaja Duleep Singh exiled, the Kohinoor taken 💎)\n- Sikh soldiers & farmers later shaped armies, farms and communities worldwide. Chardi kala! 🙏"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_british", patterns: ["east india company","british rule in india","battle of plassey","how did british come to india","british raj","company rule","land revenue systems zamindari","drain of wealth","britishers india me kaise aaye","colonial rule effects"],
        responses: ["🏴 **How a trading company ruled India (the East India Company story):**\n- **1600:** Queen Elizabeth charters the EIC to trade spices/cotton. They set up 'factories' (warehouses) — Surat, Madras, Bombay, Calcutta — with the Mughals' permission\n- **1757 Battle of Plassey:** Robert Clive defeats Bengal's Nawab Siraj-ud-Daulah (with the traitor Mir Jafar's help 🐍) → the Company becomes a KINGMAKER. 1764 Buxar → they get **Diwani rights**: collecting Bengal's taxes! A shop became a state\n- **Expansion tricks:** wars (Anglo-Mysore vs Tipu, Anglo-Maratha, Anglo-Sikh), and **'Doctrine of Lapse'** (Dalhousie: no heir? we take your kingdom — Satara, Jhansi!) & Subsidiary Alliance (your army OUT, ours IN, you pay)\n- **The economics — 'Drain of Wealth'** (Dadabhai Naoroji's calculation): raw cotton out, factory cloth in; India's world-GDP share fell from ~23% (1700) to <4% (1947). Famines (Bengal 1943: ~30 lakh dead) under export-hungry policy 💔\n- **1857 revolt** ended Company rule → the British Crown took over ('British Raj') until 1947\n- They also left railways (1853), telegraphs, English education & a unified administration — infrastructure built for THEIR profit that India repurposed for its own. History is never simple 🤔"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_bihar", patterns: ["history of bihar","magadha empire","nalanda university history","bodh gaya buddha","mahavira jainism bihar","champaran satyagraha","bihar ka itihas","vaishali republic","patna history patliputra","munger history"],
        responses: ["🏛️ **Bihar — the state that shaped civilization:**\n- Ancient name: **Magadha** — the empire heartland. Pataliputra (modern **Patna**) was one of the WORLD'S largest cities under the Mauryas & Guptas; Megasthenes wrote about its splendor. ('Bihar' comes from **viharas** — Buddhist monasteries 🛕)\n- **Spiritual ground zero:** Siddhartha attained enlightenment at **Bodh Gaya** → became the **Buddha**; **Mahavira** (24th Jain Tirthankara) was born at Kundagrama near Vaishali. Two world religions born in one state!\n- **Vaishali** — arguably the world's first REPUBLIC (Lichchhavi assembly, ~6th c. BCE) — democracy before Athens 🗳️\n- **Nalanda University** (5th–12th c.) — the world's first residential university: 10,000 students, 2,000 teachers from Tibet to Turkey, a 9-storey library; Xuanzang studied here. Its ruins are UNESCO-listed; a new Nalanda University opened in 2014!\n- **1917 Champaran Satyagraha** — Gandhi's FIRST civil disobedience in India, for indigo farmers vs British planters — it began right here (Rajendra Prasad accompanied him!) and lit the freedom fire 🔥\n- **Munger (your turf!):** the ancient Champa-nagari region, **Bihar School of Yoga** (1964, Satyananda Saraswati — yoga known worldwide), the Munger fort & Sita Charan hill, and it sits on the Ganga's south bank in the Magadh division\n- Bihar gave India its first President (Rajendra Prasad), Kalki (future!), the 'Zero' math tradition (Aryabhata worked in Kusumapura=Patna), and Bollywood's toughest actors. Gaurav se boliye — Bihari hain! 🇮🇳"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_renaissance", patterns: ["renaissance","renaissance period","leonardo da vinci","michelangelo","rebirth of learning","renaissance artists","printing press gutenberg","humanism renaissance","renaissance kya tha","when was the renaissance"],
        responses: ["🎨 **The Renaissance (~1300–1600) — Europe hits 'refresh':**\n- Meaning: **'rebirth'** — of Greek & Roman learning, art and curiosity. Born in Italy's rich cities (Florence first — the Medici bankers funded everything 💰)\n- **Humanism:** focus on human potential & this life (not only the afterlife) — 'a man can do all things if he will'\n- **The Titans:**\n  · **Leonardo da Vinci** — Mona Lisa, The Last Supper + sketching helicopters & anatomy 400 years early (the ultimate 'polymath') 🖌️\n  · **Michelangelo** — the Sistine Chapel ceiling (painted lying on scaffolding for 4 years!), David statue\n  · **Raphael** — School of Athens · **Galileo** — the telescope (science's renaissance!) 🔭\n- **Gutenberg's printing press (~1440)** = the internet of its day: books 100× cheaper → ideas (and the Reformation) spread like wildfire 📚\n- Why it matters: curiosity + evidence + art fused → led directly to the Age of Exploration and the Scientific Revolution\n- India connection: Mughal emperors later traded with & learned from Renaissance Europe (Humayun's tomb & the Taj show Persian-Indian genius; Jesuit painters influenced Mughal miniatures!) 🌍"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_ww1", patterns: ["first world war","ww1 causes","trench warfare","ww1 1914 to 1918","archduke ferdinand assassination","treaty of versailles","allied central powers ww1","pratham vishwa yudh","indian soldiers in world war 1"],
        responses: ["💥 **World War I (1914–1918) — 'The Great War':**\n- **The spark:** Archduke **Franz Ferdinand** of Austria-Hungary assassinated at Sarajevo (28 June 1914). Alliance dominoes fell in WEEKS: Austria→Serbia→Russia→Germany→France→Britain\n- **Two teams:** **Allies** (Britain, France, Russia, Italy, later USA & Japan) vs **Central Powers** (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire)\n- **New horrors:** trench warfare (mud, rats, no-man's-land), machine guns, poison gas, tanks (first used 1916), submarines, aircraft — industrial killing at scale. Verdun & the Somme: millions dead for kilometres of mud ⚰️\n- **India's role:** 1.3 MILLION Indian soldiers fought (Mesopotamia, Gallipoli, France) — 74,000 died. India Gate lists their names. The promised reforms never came → Jallianwala Bagh & the Non-Cooperation movement followed 🇮🇳\n- **End:** armistice 11 Nov 1918 (11th hour, 11th day, 11th month). **Treaty of Versailles (1919)** humiliated Germany (war guilt, crushing reparations) — historians agree it planted the seeds of WWII\n- Toll: ~20 million dead + the Spanish Flu (50M+). Four empires collapsed (German, Austro-Hungarian, Ottoman, Russian)"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_ww2", patterns: ["second world war","ww2 causes","hitler and nazis","hiroshima nagasaki","d day","axis allied powers ww2","dwitiya vishwa yudh","holocaust","when did world war 2 end"],
        responses: ["🌍 **World War II (1939–1945) — history's deadliest war (~70-85 million dead):**\n- **Causes:** Versailles bitterness + the Great Depression → fascism rises: **Hitler's Nazis** in Germany (rearmament, Lebensraum, racial ideology), Mussolini in Italy, militarist Japan in Asia\n- **Teams:** **Axis** (Germany, Italy, Japan) vs **Allies** (Britain, USSR, USA, France, China + colonies incl. India)\n- **Arc:** 1939 Germany invades Poland (Sept 1 — war starts) · Blitzkrieg sweeps Europe · **1941: Hitler betrays & invades the USSR** (Stalingrad 1942-43 = the turning bloodbath ❄️) · Japan hits **Pearl Harbor** (Dec 1941) → USA enters · **D-Day (6 June 1944)** — 156,000 Allies storm Normandy beaches 🛥️ · Germany collapses May 1945 (Hitler suicides) · **Hiroshima & Nagasaki (Aug 6 & 9, 1945)** — atomic bombs, Japan surrenders (Sep 2, 1945)\n- **The Holocaust:** the Nazis' industrialized murder of 6 million Jews + millions of others (Roma, disabled, political prisoners). 'Never again' → the Nuremberg trials invented 'crimes against humanity'\n- **India's role:** 2.5 MILLION volunteers — the largest volunteer army in history! Fought North Africa, Italy, Burma (Imphal-Kohima = 'Stalingrad of the East'). Meanwhile: the **Bengal Famine of 1943** (~30 lakh dead) & Quit India movement — the war exhausted Britain → 1947 independence 🇮🇳\n- Out of the ashes: the **United Nations** (1945), decolonization, and the Cold War"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_un", patterns: ["united nations","what is the un","un security council","who is the un secretary general","un headquarters","un agencies unicef who","why was un formed","united nations ka gathan","veto power countries","un full form"],
        responses: ["🕊️ **The United Nations (est. 24 Oct 1945, after WWII's 'never again'):**\n- **Purpose:** keep world peace, protect human rights, deliver aid, promote development. HQ: New York (that land is technically 'international'!). 193 member states — almost everyone\n- **Main organs:**\n  · **General Assembly** — all 193, one vote each (the world's parliament 🗳️)\n  · **Security Council** — 15 members: 5 PERMANENT (USA, Russia, China, UK, France — each with **VETO** power) + 10 elected. India has asked for a permanent seat for decades (fair point!)\n  · **Secretariat** — the staff, led by the **Secretary-General** (António Guterres since 2017)\n  · **International Court of Justice** — at The Hague (settles country-vs-country cases)\n- **Famous agencies:** WHO (health, eradicated smallpox!) · UNICEF (children — India's first PM Nehru accepted UNICEF aid, then India became a DONOR!) · UNESCO (World Heritage sites — the Taj's official keeper) · WFP (food aid, Nobel Peace Prize 2020) · UNHCR (refugees)\n- Criticisms: veto paralysis (Syria, Ukraine...), slow peacekeeping. Still: no world war in 80 years — the UN's quiet résumé 🌍"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "hist_wonders", patterns: ["seven wonders of the world","new seven wonders","ancient wonders","taj mahal wonder","great wall wonder","wonders of the world list","ajeebare aalam","which wonder is in india","colosseum chichen itza petra","statue of christ redeemer"],
        responses: ["🗿 **The Wonders — humanity's flex list:**\n\n**New 7 Wonders (global vote, 2007, 100M+ voters!):**\n1. 🇮🇳 **Taj Mahal**, Agra (1632-53) — India's entry, built by Shah Jahan for Mumtaz\n2. 🇨🇳 **Great Wall** — 21,000 km, 2,000 years of construction\n3. 🇧🇷 **Christ the Redeemer**, Rio — 30m statue, arms open over the city\n4. 🇵🇪 **Machu Picchu** — the Inca sky-city in the Andes\n5. 🇲🇽 **Chichén Itzá** — Maya pyramid where the equinox sun makes a serpent shadow 🐍\n6. 🇮🇹 **Colosseum**, Rome — 50,000-seat gladiator arena\n7. 🇯🇴 **Petra** — a city carved into rose-red cliffs\n(+ the **Great Pyramid of Giza** — honorary wonder, the ONLY ancient one still standing)\n\n**Ancient 7 (the original Greek list):** Great Pyramid 🏆 · Hanging Gardens of Babylon (maybe mythical?) · Statue of Zeus at Olympia · Temple of Artemis · Mausoleum at Halicarnassus (gave us the word 'mausoleum'!) · Colossus of Rhodes · Lighthouse of Alexandria — all gone except Giza. Earthquakes & time: the original undefeated champion 🌪️\n\nModern contenders: Statue of Unity (world's tallest, 182m 🇮🇳), Burj Khalifa (828m), Panama Canal..."] });

    /* ══════════ SST — GEOGRAPHY deeper ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "geo_bihar", patterns: ["bihar state information","about bihar","bihar geography","rivers of bihar","districts of bihar","munger bihar","bihar ka bhugol","bihar population literacy","gandhi setu bihar","capital of bihar patna details"],
        responses: ["🌾 **Bihar — your home state, by the numbers:**\n- **Capital:** Patna (ancient Pataliputra — 2,500+ years continuously inhabited, one of the world's oldest capitals!)\n- **Location:** eastern India, Gangetic plain; bordered by UP (west), Nepal 🇳🇵 (north), West Bengal (east), Jharkhand (south)\n- **38 districts** · 3 divisions of relevance to you: **Munger** is a district AND division HQ on the Ganga's south bank — neighbours: Bhagalpur, Jamui, Lakhisarai, Khagaria, Sheikhpura\n- **Rivers:** the **Ganga** flows right through (Patna→Munger→Bhagalpur); tributaries: Gandak, Bagmati, Kamla, Kosi ('Sorrow of Bihar' — it shifts course & floods 🌊), Sone, Punpun\n- **Climate:** humid subtropical — hot summers (45°C peaks!), monsoon June-Sept, cool winters; occasional floods north of the Ganga, droughts south\n- **Economy:** India's most agricultural state — litchi (Muzaffarpur's GI-tagged **Shahi litchi**! 🍒), mangoes, makhana (fox nuts — 90% of world supply from Mithila!), rice-wheat-sugarcane\n- **People:** ~13 crore (3rd most populated state), densest in India (~1,100/km²), youngest state (median age ~20!) — Hindi + Bhojpuri, Maithili, Magahi, Angika, Bajjika\n- **Landmarks:** Bodh Gaya (UNESCO), Nalanda ruins (UNESCO), Mahabodhi temple, Takht Sri Patna Sahib (Guru Gobind Singh's birthplace 🙏), Valmiki Tiger Reserve, Vikramshila ruins, Munger Fort + Bihar School of Yoga, Sita Charan hill\n- Bihar = Buddha + books (Nalanda!) + bravery (1857's Pir Ali, the 1974 JP movement) — ancient glory, modern hustle 💪"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "geo_parks", patterns: ["national parks of india","wildlife sanctuaries","jim corbett national park","kaziranga one horned rhino","tiger reserves india","project tiger","biosphere reserves","wildlife sanctuary vs national park","ranthambore tigers","sundarbans delta"],
        responses: ["🐅 **India's protected wild places:**\n- **National Parks** (strictest: no human activity) — India has **106** · **Wildlife Sanctuaries** (protection with some rights) — **570+** · **Tiger Reserves** — 54 · **Biosphere Reserves** — 18\n- **Famous national parks:**\n  · **Jim Corbett** (Uttarakhand, 1936 — India's FIRST! Project Tiger launched here 1973) 🐅\n  · **Kaziranga** (Assam) — ⅔ of the world's **one-horned rhinos** 🦏\n  · **Ranthambore** (Rajasthan) — tigers stalking under a 1000-year-old fort\n  · **Sundarbans** (WB) — the world's largest mangrove delta, SWIMMING tigers, UNESCO 🐆\n  · **Periyar** (Kerala) — elephants by the lake · **Gir** (Gujarat) — the ONLY home of Asiatic lions 🦁\n  · **Hemis** (Ladakh) — India's largest; snow leopards 🐆❄️\n  · **Valmiki** (West Champaran) — Bihar's only national park & tiger reserve! 🇮🇳\n  · Kanha (Mowgli's real-life 'Seoni hills' inspiration for The Jungle Book 📖), Bandhavgarh (highest tiger density), Dudhwa, Manas, Great Himalayan NP\n- **Success story:** tigers 1,827 (2010) → **3,167 (2022)** — India holds 75% of the world's tigers 📈\n- Birders' paradise: Keoladeo/Bharatpur (UNESCO), Chilika lake (dolphins!), Pulicat"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "geo_dams", patterns: ["dams of india","bhakra nangal dam","hirakud dam","largest dam in india","why are dams built","sardar sarovar dam","dam irrigation hydroelectric","tehri dam","narmada dams","bandh kya hai"],
        responses: ["🌊 **Dams — India's water batteries:**\n- **Why build them?** irrigation (farms drink first), **hydroelectricity** (falling water spins turbines — clean power!), flood control, drinking water\n- **The famous ones:**\n  · **Bhakra Nangal** (Sutlej river, HP/Punjab border, 1963) — Nehru called dams 'the temples of modern India'; its Gobind Sagar reservoir fed the Green Revolution 🌾\n  · **Hirakud** (Mahanadi, Odisha, 1957) — the **longest earthen dam in the world** (25.8 km!)\n  · **Tehri** (Bhagirathi, Uttarakhand) — India's TALLEST dam (260m)\n  · **Sardar Sarovar** (Narmada, Gujarat) — second-largest concrete volume on Earth; feeds 4 states\n  · **Nagarjuna Sagar** (Krishna) — once the world's tallest masonry dam · **Bakras/Nathpa**, **Koyna**, **Srisailam** (biggest hydro capacity)\n- **In Bihar:** farmland rides rivers more than big dams, but the **Barauni-Kosi canal systems**, Ajan Dam (Jamui), Karamdara (Munger!), Nagi/Nakti dams (Jamui — bird sanctuaries!) matter locally. The Kosi barrage & embankments = flood control for north Bihar 🚧\n- **The debate:** dams displace villages, trap silt (deltas shrink!), block fish migration — Narmada Bachao Andolan (Medha Patkar) made India balance development vs rights. Modern answer: better planning + solar/wind mix ⚖️"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "geo_industries", patterns: ["industries of india","iron and steel industry","cotton textile industry mumbai","it industry bangalore","industrial regions of india","jute industry kolkata","major industries of india","make in india","udhyog ke bare me","why is jamshedpur called steel city"],
        responses: ["🏭 **India's industrial map:**\n- **Iron & Steel** (the backbone): **Jamshedpur** — 'Steel City', born 1907 with TISCO (now Tata Steel), India's first! 🇮🇳 Near it: Bokaro (Jharkhand), Bhilai (Chhattisgarh), Rourkela (Odisha), Durgapur (WB), Burnpur — all near coal/iron belts (why Jharkhand-Odisha-Bengal = India's Ruhr Valley!) **Salem, Vijayanagar** in the south\n- **Cotton textiles:** Mumbai ('cottonopolis' — humid climate keeps thread from snapping!), Ahmedabad ('Manchester of India'), Surat, Coimbatore, Ludhiana (woollens). Handloom: Banarasi silk 🥻, Bhagalpur silk (Tussar — YOUR region's pride!), Kanjivaram\n- **Jute:** the Hooghly basin (Kolkata) — golden fibre, world's biggest jute belt\n- **IT & software:** **Bengaluru** ('Silicon Valley of India' 🖥️), Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai, Gurgaon, Noida — India = the world's back office + code kitchen; TCS/Infosys/Wipro are global giants\n- **Auto:** Chennai-Detroit of Asia, Pune, Gurugram (Maruti), Jamshedpur (Tata trucks)\n- **Refining:** Jamnagar (world's LARGEST refinery — Reliance!), Panipat, Mathura, Barauni (Bihar's refinery!) 🛢️\n- **New push:** 'Make in India' — electronics (iPhone assembly in India now!), defence corridors, semiconductor mission (first fab coming to Gujarat), PLI schemes\n- Industry = jobs + exports + pride. Bihar's frontier: food processing (makhana!), ethanol, textile parks 🌱"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "geo_transport", patterns: ["transport in india","indian railways facts","largest railway network","national highways india","waterways in india","major ports of india","airports in india","railway zones","parivahan ke sadhan","indian railways daily passengers"],
        responses: ["🚆 **India on the move — the transport web:**\n- **Railways (the lifeline!):** 4th largest network globally (~68,000 km), **~2.3 crore passengers DAILY** (≈ Australia's whole population, every day!), ~13,000+ trains. 1853: first train Mumbai→Thane (34 km). Now: **Vande Bharat** (180 km/h semi-high-speed, made in India 🇮🇳), Gatimaan, dedicated freight corridors. Mountain railways (Darjeeling, Nilgiri, Kalka-Shimla) are UNESCO 'toy train' heritage! Patna Junction is one of the busiest in the east\n- **Roads:** 2nd largest network (~6.3 million km!). Golden Quadrilateral (Delhi-Mumbai-Chennai-Kolkata), 60+ national highway projects, expressways (Yamuna, Delhi-Mumbai 1,380km coming!)\n- **Air:** UDAN scheme made small-city flights (Patna, Gaya, Darbhanga airports buzzing ✈️); busiest: Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru\n- **Water:** 111 national waterways — **NW-1 = the Ganga** (Varanasi→Haldia; your Munger sits on it! 🚢), 12 major ports (Mumbai, JNPT, Kandla, Chennai, Paradip...), plus ancient coastal trade\n- **Metro:** Delhi (one of the world's longest!), Mumbai, Bengaluru, Kolkata (India's FIRST, 1984), Lucknow, Kochi, Jaipur, Nagpur, Patna & Bhojpur-line metros planned/underway 🚇\n- Every rupee of transport multiplies the economy — that's why 'gati shakti' (speed-power) is the buzzword!"] });

    /* ══════════ SST — CIVICS deeper ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "civics_duties", patterns: ["11 fundamental duties","article 51a","citizen duties list","duty of every indian","kartavya kya hain","fundamental duties class 8","swarni jayanti duties","duty vs rights"],
        responses: ["🫡 **The 11 Fundamental Duties (Article 51A — added 1976, on the Swaran Singh Committee's advice):**\nEvery Indian citizen SHOULD:\n1. Follow the Constitution, respect the **National Flag & Anthem** 🇮🇳\n2. Cherish the ideals of the freedom struggle\n3. Uphold India's **sovereignty, unity & integrity**\n4. **Defend the country** when called\n5. Promote **harmony & brotherhood** beyond religion/region/language; renounce practices derogatory to women's dignity\n6. Preserve our **composite culture** (the Nalanda-to-Naatu-Natu heritage!)\n7. Protect the **environment** — forests, lakes, rivers, wildlife; have compassion 🌳\n8. Develop **scientific temper & spirit of inquiry** (question, verify, learn!)\n9. Safeguard **public property**; abjure violence\n10. Strive for **excellence** in all spheres\n11. (Added 2002) Provide **education** to your child/ward aged 6–14\n\nThe logic: rights and duties are two wheels of the same bicycle 🚲 — a democracy where everyone claims rights but nobody does duties crashes. Fun test: which duty did YOU do today? (I'd say #8 — you're literally interrogating an AI 😄)"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "civics_amendments", patterns: ["important constitutional amendments","42nd amendment mini constitution","73rd 74th amendment","86th amendment right to education","101st amendment gst","amendment process constitution","how is constitution amended","44th amendment","103rd amendment ews","constitutional amendment kya hai"],
        responses: ["📜 **How the Constitution changes + the amendments that shaped India:**\n- **Process (Article 368):** Parliament passes a Bill by **special majority** (⅔ of members present + majority of total strength); some need state ratification too; then the President MUST sign. It's deliberately hard — you can't rewrite the rulebook on a whim! (Basic structure can NEVER be amended — the Supreme Court's Kesavananda Bharati ruling, 1973 ⚖️)\n- **Landmark amendments:**\n  · **1st (1951)** — land reform vs right to property tussle\n  · **42nd (1976)** — the 'MINI-CONSTITUTION': added 'socialist, secular, integrity' to the Preamble + the Fundamental Duties (during the Emergency — controversial!)\n  · **44th (1978)** — rolled back Emergency excesses; property right demoted\n  · **61st (1988)** — voting age **21→18** 🗳️ (your future vote was born here!)\n  · **73rd & 74th (1992)** — gave Panchayats & Municipalities constitutional power + ⅓ women's reservation\n  · **86th (2002)** — **Right to Education** (free & compulsory, ages 6-14) became a fundamental right 📚\n  · **101st (2016)** — **GST**: 'One Nation, One Tax' 🧾\n  · **103rd (2019)** — 10% EWS reservation\n  · **104th/105th** — SC/ST quota extension & OBC list powers\n- ~106 amendments in 75 years — the Constitution is a living document, updating like good software 🔄"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "civics_rti", patterns: ["right to information act","rti kya hai","how to file an rti","rti application process","rti act 2005","information commission","why is rti powerful","rti fees","ask government questions rti"],
        responses: ["🔍 **RTI (Right to Information Act, 2005) — YOUR 'show me the files' power:**\n- Any citizen can ask ANY government office (central, state, PSUs, even NGOs on govt funds): **'What did you do, why, and show me the records.'** They must reply in **30 days**\n- **How:** plain-paper application (or online at rtionline.gov.in for central govt) → address it to the **'Public Information Officer'** of that department → ask specific questions ('certified copy of…', 'daily progress made on…', 'name the officials responsible for…') → fee ₹10 (free for BPL; free if they're late!) → submit by post/online/in person\n- **Why it's feared & loved:** RTI exposed scams (Adarsh, CWG…), got roads built, pensions released, ration delivered — lakhs of ordinary Indians use it yearly. It turned 'sarkar ka kaam' from a black box into a glass box 🫙\n- **Limits:** security/intelligence agencies (Schedule 2), privacy of others, cabinet papers — but corruption info is NEVER exempt\n- **First appeal** if refused/ignored → appellate officer (30 days) → **Information Commission** (the RTI judges can fine officers ₹250/day!) ⚖️\n- It's a fundamental-right cousin (Article 19's freedom of speech includes 'right to KNOW'). Democracy = you're the boss; RTI = your audit tool 🧾"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "civics_consumer", patterns: ["consumer rights","consumer protection act","how to complain against a shop","isi mark","consumer forum","right to be informed as consumer","mrp rules","expired product complaint","upbhokta adhikar","national consumer helpline"],
        responses: ["🛒 **Consumer rights — the customer IS king (legally!):**\n- **Consumer Protection Act 2019** (updating the 1986 act) gives you 6 rights:\n  1. **Safety** (no dangerous goods) 2. **Information** (ingredients, price, expiry, MRP) 3. **Choice** 4. **Being heard** 5. **Redressal** (compensation!) 6. **Consumer education**\n- **MRP is LAW:** overcharging above MRP = punishable. No 'packaging charges' tricks on packaged goods\n- **Marks to trust:** ISI (electronics), FSSAI number (food), AGMARK (farm produce), Hallmark (gold purity!), BIS ✅ — buy marked, complain unmarked\n- **How to fight back (cheapest→strongest):**\n  1. Bill + photo evidence → complain to the shop/company first\n  2. **National Consumer Helpline: 1915** (call/WhatsApp/app) — free govt mediator 📞\n  3. **e-Daakhil portal** — file a case in Consumer Commission online (District → State → National; claims up to ₹50L start at District level; NO lawyer needed, NO court fee for small cases!)\n  4. Food safety → FSSAI complaint; ads lie → ASCI\n- Landmark wins: students got exam fee refunds, patients got crores for negligence, food-delivery apps got fined\n- **Golden rule:** ALWAYS take the bill. No bill = no proof = no justice 🧾⚖️"] });

    /* ══════════ SST — ECONOMICS deeper ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "econ_planning", patterns: ["five year plans of india","planning commission","niti aayog","first five year plan","planning in india","panchvarshiy yojana","why was planning commission replaced","mixed economy india","harrod domar model plan"],
        responses: ["🗓️ **India's planning story — Five-Year Plans to NITI Aayog:**\n- **Why plan?** In 1950 India was poor, farm-dependent & industry-empty. A **mixed economy** was chosen: government builds the big bones (dams, steel, rail), private enterprise builds the flesh\n- **Planning Commission (1950)** — chaired by the PM; made the **Five-Year Plans** (a Soviet idea adapted desi-style):\n  · **1st Plan (1951-56)** — agriculture & dams (Harrod-Domar model)\n  · **2nd (1956-61)** — heavy industry (Mahalanobis model): Bhilai, Rourkela, Durgapur steel plants 🏭\n  · **3rd** — self-reliance (hit by wars + drought; 'Plan Holiday' followed)\n  · **Green Revolution** plans made India food-surplus by the 70s 🌾\n  · **1991:** economy nearly defaulted → liberalization (the 8th plan era): licences abolished, markets opened\n  · **12th (2012-17)** — the LAST five-year plan\n- **2015: Planning Commission → NITI Aayog** ('National Institution for Transforming India') — new philosophy: no top-down plans; instead think-tank + **cooperative federalism** (states as partners), 15-year vision docs, aspirational-districts competitions\n- Legacy: planning built IITs, ISRO, the steel cities, and the habit of thinking long-term. The baton just changed shape 🏃"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "econ_globalization", patterns: ["what is globalization","liberalization 1991 india","privatization LPG reforms","wto world trade organization","multinational companies","globalization effects on india","1991 economic reforms manmohan","foreign investment india","vaishvikaran kya hai","global village"],
        responses: ["🌐 **Globalization — the world becoming one big market:**\n- **Definition:** freer flow of goods, money, technology, ideas & people across borders — the 'global village' 🏘️🌍\n- **India's turning point — 1991 ('LPG reforms'):** India had just ~2 weeks of foreign-exchange left 😱. Finance Minister Manmohan Singh + PM Narasimha Rao opened the doors:\n  · **L**iberalization — licence raj ended (no more permits-to-produce!), tariffs slashed\n  · **P**rivatization — PSUs got competition; airlines, telecom, banking opened to private players\n  · **G**lobalization — foreign companies & investment welcomed (FDI)\n- **Results:** forex $1.2B (1991) → $650B+ today 📈 · GDP growth leapt to 7-9% · IT/software boom (Bengaluru!) · cars, phones, channels, brands everywhere · a huge middle class rose — AND inequality widened, small industries faced tough competition, farm distress persisted (the honest side ⚖️)\n- **WTO (1995)** — the trade referee: 164 members, sets rules, settles disputes (India has won cases against the US!)\n- **MNCs** — companies operating worldwide (Samsung, Coca-Cola, Tata itself — Jaguar Land Rover is INDIAN-owned!)\n- Your phone: designed in California, chip from Taiwan, assembled in India, apps by Indians — THAT's globalization, in your pocket 📱"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "econ_budget", patterns: ["union budget","what is the budget of india","fiscal deficit","budget 1 february","railway budget merged","revenue and capital expenditure","budget kya hota hai","finance minister budget speech","budget terms explained","deficit meaning"],
        responses: ["💼 **The Union Budget — India's yearly balance sheet:**\n- **What:** the government's annual plan of **income** (taxes, dividends) vs **spending** (salaries, schemes, defence, interest). Presented in Parliament on **1 February** since 2017 (earlier: last day of Feb; the Railway Budget merged into it in 2016 after 92 years! 🚂)\n- **Who:** the **Finance Minister** reads the speech (from a 'bahi khata' ledger now — a nice desi touch 📕); it must PASS in Parliament (that's democracy controlling the money)\n- **Key terms decoded:**\n  · **Revenue expenditure** = daily running costs (salaries, subsidies, pensions)\n  · **Capital expenditure (capex)** = asset-building (roads, railways, schools, defence hardware) — the 'good spending' economists cheer 🏗️\n  · **Fiscal deficit** = total spending − total income (excluding borrowings), as % of GDP. India targets ~4.5% → it's the extra money the government borrows\n  · **Revenue deficit** = borrowing just to run daily life (bad sign!)\n  · **Cess** = tax-on-tax for a specific purpose (education, health)\n- **State budgets** exist too (Bihar's budget covers Patna metro, liquor-ban enforcement, student credits!)\n- Budget day = TV debates, tax changes, meme fest. Now you can actually follow it 😎📊"] });

    /* ══════════ ICT — Computer science & digital ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_history", patterns: ["history of computers","who invented the computer","charles babbage","first computer in the world","abacus to supercomputer","computer generations","ada lovelace first programmer","eniac computer","evolution of computers","computer ka itihas"],
        responses: ["🖥️ **The computer's journey — from beads to brains:**\n- **~2500 BCE: Abacus** — the first 'computing device' (beads on wires; still faster than calculators for kids trained on it!)\n- **1822–1837: Charles Babbage** — 'Father of the Computer' — designed the **Difference Engine** & the **Analytical Engine** (had memory, a 'mill' processor, punch-card input — a computer 100 years before electronics!). **Ada Lovelace** wrote its algorithm → the **world's first programmer** 👩‍💻\n- **1936: Alan Turing** — the 'Turing machine' concept (the maths of computation itself)\n- **1946: ENIAC** — first general-purpose electronic computer: 30 tonnes, 1,800 sq ft, 140 kW — less power than your phone 📱\n- **The 5 generations:**\n  1. **Vacuum tubes** (1940s-50s) — ENIAC, UNIVAC (room-sized, hot, failed often)\n  2. **Transistors** (1956-63) — smaller, cooler, reliable\n  3. **Integrated circuits** (1964-71) — chips!\n  4. **Microprocessors** (1971-now) — Intel 4004 put a CPU on ONE chip → personal computers (Apple II, IBM PC 1981)\n  5. **AI & ULSI** (now) — parallel processing, quantum, neural networks — you're talking to gen-5 right now 🤖\n- India's marks: **TIFRAC (1956)** first Indian computer; PARAM supercomputers; ISRO's onboard computers; and Indian engineers power much of the world's tech! 🇮🇳"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_units", patterns: ["computer memory units","bit byte kilobyte","how many bytes in a kb","storage units explained","ram rom difference","binary system 0 and 1","ascii code","memory hierarchy cache","1 gb equals how many mb","computer units class 8"],
        responses: ["💾 **Computer units — the digital measuring scale:**\n- **Bit** = 1 or 0 (the atom of data) · **Byte** = 8 bits (one letter, like 'A' = 01000001 in ASCII)\n- The ladder (each ×1024 = 2¹⁰):\n  · 1 **KB** (kilobyte) = 1,024 bytes ≈ a short paragraph\n  · 1 **MB** = 1,024 KB ≈ an MP3 song 🎵\n  · 1 **GB** = 1,024 MB ≈ an HD movie 🎬\n  · 1 **TB** = 1,024 GB ≈ 500 movies / your whole photo life\n  · then PB, EB, ZB, YB… (the internet moves in zettabytes!)\n- **RAM vs ROM:** RAM = the desk you work on (fast, temporary, wiped when power goes — 'volatile') 🪑 · ROM = the bookshelf of permanent instructions (BIOS/firmware, survives power-off)\n- **Memory hierarchy (speed vs size):** Registers → Cache (L1/L2/L3, superfast tiny) → RAM → SSD → HDD → cloud/tape. The CPU is a genius with amnesia — caches are its sticky notes 📝\n- **Binary:** computers only understand on/off electrical states = 1/0. Decimal 13 = 1101₂ (8+4+1). Everything — photos, videos, this conversation — is billions of 1s and 0s. Mind = blown? 🤯"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_input_output", patterns: ["input and output devices","types of input devices","output devices examples","storage devices computer","mouse keyboard printer scanner","hardware vs software","types of software","system software application software","input output storage devices list","computer hardware parts"],
        responses: ["⌨️ **Input / Output / Storage — the computer's senses, voice & memory:**\n- **Input devices (data IN):** keyboard, mouse, touchpad, **touchscreen** (both in AND out!), microphone 🎤, scanner, webcam, joystick, light pen, OMR (exam sheets!), MICR (bank cheques), barcode reader, biometric (your fingerprint = input!)\n- **Output devices (results OUT):** monitor (LED/OLED), printer (inkjet/laser/3D!), speakers, headphones, projector, plotter (huge blueprints), braille printer\n- **Storage (memory that persists):** pen drives, SD cards, HDDs, **SSDs** (no moving parts — 10× faster!), CDs/DVDs/Blu-ray, and **cloud storage** (Google Drive — your Fair Copies folder lives there ☁️)\n- **Hardware vs software:** hardware = what you can kick 😄 (physical parts: CPU, monitor, RAM) · software = the instructions that tell hardware what to do\n- **Software's two families:**\n  · **System software** — runs the machine: OS (Windows, Android, Linux, iOS), device drivers, compilers\n  · **Application software** — does YOUR work: Word, Chrome, games, BitBot's site! 🎮\n- The IPO cycle: **Input → Process (CPU + RAM) → Output**, with storage keeping everything safe between sessions 🔄"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_office", patterns: ["ms word","microsoft excel formulas","powerpoint presentation tips","what is ms office","spreadsheet basics","word processor features","excel charts pivot","office suite comparison google docs","ms office full form","documents spreadsheet presentation software"],
        responses: ["📊 **MS Office — the desktop toolkit (and its free rivals):**\n- **Word** 📝 — word processing: reports, letters, bios. Pro moves: Styles (auto table-of-contents!), Ctrl+B/I/U, mail merge (1 letter → 1000 personalized)\n- **Excel** 🔢 — spreadsheets: data in rows×columns, and FORMULAS do the maths:\n  · `=SUM(A1:A10)` totals · `=AVERAGE(...)` · `=MAX/MIN` · `=COUNT` · `=IF(A1>33,\"Pass\",\"Fail\")`\n  · `=VLOOKUP(...)` hunts values across tables · PivotTables summarize 1 lakh rows in 2 clicks · Charts make data sing 📈\n  · Cell A1 = column A, row 1; `$A$1` locks it when copying\n- **PowerPoint** 🖥️ — presentations: one idea per slide, 6×6 rule (6 lines, 6 words), big images > walls of text\n- **Others:** Outlook (mail/calendar), OneNote (notes), Access (databases)\n- **Free alternatives:** **Google Docs/Sheets/Slides** (browser, auto-saves, real-time collab — your school projects probably live here!), LibreOffice, WPS\n- Shortcut of the day: Ctrl+S (save), Ctrl+Z (undo — the time machine ⏪), Ctrl+Shift+T (reopen closed tab). Master Excel and half the office world's jobs open up 💼"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_email", patterns: ["what is email","how email works","smtp pop3 imap","email etiquette","gmail features","cc bcc meaning","email address parts","spam and phishing email","compose email rules","email kaise likhein"],
        responses: ["📧 **Email — the internet's original killer app (still #1!):**\n- **Address anatomy:** `bitbot@bittuhere.dev` = username **@** domain. The '@' = 'at that server'\n- **How it flows:** your mail app → **SMTP** server (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol — the postman, sends mail, port 587) → recipient's server → they fetch it via **POP3** (download & delete — old school) or **IMAP** (syncs everywhere — phone+laptop see the same mail ✅)\n- **Parts:** To / **CC** (carbon copy — everyone sees who got it) / **BCC** (blind CC — hidden recipients, great for bulk mail without exposing addresses!) / Subject (the headline — write it LAST but make it count) / Body / Attachments\n- **Etiquette:** short subject ('Quiz doubt — Q14'), salutation, short paragraphs, polite sign-off, no ALL CAPS (= SHOUTING), reply-all only when everyone truly needs it, attach before sending (we've ALL hit send too early 😅)\n- **Dangers:** **Spam** (junk — Gmail filters 100+ billion/day!) · **Phishing** (fake 'your account is blocked' mails stealing passwords — check the sender domain, never click!) · never open weird attachments\n- The site's Contact form? Basically a styled email sender! And your Weekly Quiz ban notices would arrive by email too 📬"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_egovernance", patterns: ["e governance","digital india","aadhaar card upi digilocker","government online services india","digi locker","umang app","e governance services g2c","digital payments india","online government schemes portals","egovernance kya hai"],
        responses: ["🏛️ **E-Governance — government at smartphone speed:**\n- **Definition:** using IT to deliver government services — transparently, cheaply, 24×7. Types: **G2C** (govt→citizen), G2B (business), G2G (internal)\n- **India's digital stack (the world's largest!):**\n  · **Aadhaar** — 130+ crore biometric IDs, the biggest ID system on Earth 🪪\n  · **UPI** — ₹ lakh-crores monthly; even street vendors have QR codes (Kenya, France, UAE adopted it!)\n  · **DigiLocker** — official cloud for marksheets, licences, certificates (100% legal!) 📁\n  · **UMANG app** — 1,000+ govt services in one app\n  · **GeM portal** (govt buys online), **Income Tax e-filing**, **Passport Seva**, **IRCTC** (world's biggest ticket booking!), land records (Bihar's **Bhulekh/Jamabandi** portal), scholarship portals, **CoWIN** (vaccinated 200+ crore doses with digital records! 💉)\n- **Digital India mission (2015):** broadband to villages, cashless economy, e-education (DIKSHA app!) — the goal: 'faceless, paperless, cashless' governance\n- Why it matters: middlemen die, files move in minutes not months, corruption shrinks where code watches 👀\n- Homework for the future: digital divide (rural connectivity, elderly users), data privacy (DPDP Act 2023 now exists), cyber security 🛡️"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_upi", patterns: ["upi full form","how upi works","npci upi","gpay phonepe paytm upi","upi id explained","upi transaction limit","upi vs wallet","upi kaam kaise karta hai","bhim app upi"],
        responses: ["📲 **UPI — India's payment revolution (the world studies this!):**\n- **Full form: Unified Payments Interface** — built by **NPCI** (National Payments Corporation of India, owned by RBI + banks), launched **2016**\n- **The magic:** link ANY bank account → pay via a **UPI ID** (like `bittu@okhdfcbank`) or QR scan. Money moves **bank-to-bank in ~1 second**, 24×7, free for users 💸\n- **How it works:** You (payer app: GPay/PhonePe/Paytm/BHIM) → UPI switch (NPCI) → your bank & their bank settle instantly. No card numbers, no IFSC hunting, no 'wallet' middle layer\n- **QR codes:** static (vendor prints once) & dynamic (amount embedded). 'Scan & pay' killed the cash-only era 🔄\n- **Scale:** **16+ billion transactions per MONTH** (2025) — more digital payments than the US+EU combined. UPI Lite for small offline-ish taps, AutoPay for subscriptions, **UPI goes global** — France's Eiffel Tower shops, UAE, Singapore (linked with their PayNow!), Nepal & Bhutan accept it 🌍\n- **Safety:** never share your UPI PIN (it's only ever ENTERED to pay, never to RECEIVE — anyone asking your PIN to 'send you money' = scammer 🚨), check the name before confirming, ₹1L/day typical limit (₹5L for hospital/education)\n- Fun fact: even your favourite game site's creator probably has a UPI QR somewhere 😄"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_social", patterns: ["social media","history of social media","social media platforms","instagram youtube whatsapp","social media addiction","social media pros and cons","influencer culture","reels shorts viral","social media ki lat","how social media algorithms work"],
        responses: ["📱 **Social media — humanity's shared attention machine:**\n- **Timeline:** SixDegrees (1997, first) → Orkut (India's first love 💙) → Facebook (2004) → YouTube (2005) → Twitter (2006) → WhatsApp (2009) → Instagram (2010) → Snapchat stories → **TikTok → Reels/Shorts** (the 15-second era) → Threads, Discord, communities\n- **How the feed works:** algorithms rank posts by predicted engagement (watch-time, likes, shares, comments). The more you watch, the more it feeds. That's why 'one more reel' becomes 47 more reels ⏰\n- **Pros:** connection across distances, free education (YouTube = the world's university 🎓), small creators can go big (village chefs to coders!), emergency info, business/marketing for tiny shops\n- **Cons:** addiction & doomscrolling, comparison anxiety, fake news spreads 6× faster than truth (MIT study!), cyberbullying, privacy mining, shortened attention spans\n- **Class-8 survival guide:**\n  1. Time-box it (phone settings have screen-time tools — USE them)\n  2. Follow learning accounts, unfollow rage-bait\n  3. Never share OTPs, location, school details; private account ≠ private screenshot\n  4. Before forwarding: check the source (fake news = real harm)\n  5. Real friends > follower count\n- Social media is fire 🔥 — cooks food or burns the house. Your choice which chef you become!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_cyberlaw", patterns: ["cyber law india","cyber crime","it act 2000","cyber safety rules","hacking punishment india","online fraud complaint","cyber crime helpline 1930","internet safety for students","password safety tips","what is cyberbullying law"],
        responses: ["🛡️ **Cyber law & cyber safety — India's digital rulebook:**\n- **IT Act, 2000** (+2008 amendment) — India's cyber constitution: gives legal value to e-documents & digital signatures, and PUNISHES cybercrime:\n  · **Sec 66** — hacking: up to 3 yrs + ₹5 lakh fine\n  · **Sec 66C/66D** — identity theft & impersonation cheats\n  · **Sec 66E** — privacy violation (secret photos/videos)\n  · **Sec 67** — obscene content · **Sec 67B** — child-related content (harshest!)\n  · Plus BNS (Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita) sections for cheating/intimidation online\n- **DPDP Act 2023** — companies must protect your data, kids' data needs parental consent\n- **If you're hit:** 🚨 **Call 1930** (cybercrime financial helpline — report within the golden hour to freeze money!) · report at **cybercrime.gov.in** · local cyber cell FIR\n- **Student safety rules (memorize these!):**\n  1. Strong unique passwords + **2FA everywhere** (your site's 2-step change-password flow — same idea!)\n  2. Never share OTP/password — not even with 'bank officers' (they NEVER ask)\n  3. Unknown links/APKs = traps; 'free diamonds/redeem code' = 100% scam\n  4. Cyberbullying is a CRIME — screenshot evidence, tell an adult, report\n  5. Anything you post is forever — your future self (and employers!) will read it\n  6. Public Wi-Fi: no banking. HTTPS-only\n- Best firewall = an alert brain 🧠⚡"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_opensource", patterns: ["open source software","what is linux","free software gnu","open source vs proprietary","examples of open source","github open source","apache license mit","linux distributions","open source kya hai","why use open source"],
        responses: ["🐧 **Open source — software built in the open:**\n- **Definition:** software whose **source code is public** — anyone can read, use, modify & share it (usually free too). Opposite: proprietary/closed (Windows, iOS — you only get the compiled app)\n- **The legends:**\n  · **Linux** (Linus Torvalds, 1991, started as a student hobby!) — now runs ALL top-500 supercomputers, 90%+ of servers, every **Android phone** 📱, the ISS! Distributions ('distros'): Ubuntu, Fedora, Debian, Kali (hacking lab), Android\n  · **Git & GitHub** — version control (track every change, merge teamwork) — THIS VERY SITE is on GitHub Pages, open to the world! 🌐\n  · Firefox, VLC (plays ANYTHING), Blender (Hollywood-grade 3D, free!), VS Code, Python, WordPress (43% of all websites!), LibreOffice\n- **Why it wins:** free 💸, transparent (no hidden spy code), secure (1000 eyes find bugs — 'Linus's Law'), you can fork it & make your own version, no vendor lock-in\n- **GNU project (1983, Richard Stallman)** — the philosophy that started it: software freedom (free as in speech, not just beer 🍺)\n- **Licenses:** MIT/Apache (do almost anything), GPL (share your changes too — 'copyleft')\n- Careers: contributing to open source on GitHub is the BEST résumé for a developer. Your first 'hello world' pull request awaits! 🚀"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_cloud", patterns: ["cloud computing","aws azure google cloud","iaas paas saas","how does the cloud work","cloud types public private","serverless computing","cloud gaming","badal computing kya hai","where is data stored in cloud"],
        responses: ["☁️ **Cloud computing — other people's computers (in the best way):**\n- **What it really is:** instead of running software/storing files on YOUR device, you use someone else's giant data centres over the internet, **paying like electricity** (by use). 'The cloud' = real buildings full of real servers 🏢💨\n- **The 3 service layers:**\n  · **IaaS** (Infrastructure) — rent raw machines (AWS EC2)\n  · **PaaS** (Platform) — rent the environment, write code (Heroku)\n  · **SaaS** (Software) — just use the app: **Gmail, Google Drive, Docs** ← your Fair Copies & quiz scores live in this layer!\n- **The big 3:** **AWS** (Amazon, ~31% market), **Microsoft Azure**, **Google Cloud** — plus Indian players & Firebase (Google's app backend)\n- **Deployment types:** public (shared, cheap), private (your own), hybrid, community\n- **Why everyone moved:** scale instantly (a video goes viral? add 10,000 servers in minutes!), auto-backup, work from any device, no buying expensive hardware\n- **Fun facts:** cloud gaming (GeForce Now, Xbox Cloud) streams games like Netflix 🎮; India has data-residency rules; the 'edge' brings cloud closer to you (CDNs)\n- Every 'saved to Drive' is a tiny miracle of distributed systems ✨"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_ai", patterns: ["artificial intelligence","machine learning","how does chatgpt work","neural networks","ai in daily life","generative ai","deepfakes","ai ka matlab","future of ai jobs"],
        responses: ["🤖 **AI — machines that learn (and yes, that's ME!):**\n- **Artificial Intelligence:** making computers do tasks needing human-like intelligence — recognizing, deciding, predicting, generating\n- **The stack:**\n  · **AI** (the dream) ⊃ **Machine Learning** (learning patterns from data instead of hardcoded rules) ⊃ **Deep Learning** (neural networks with many layers — inspired by brain neurons!) ⊃ **Generative AI** (creates NEW text/images/audio — ChatGPT, Gemini, image generators)\n- **How LLMs like ChatGPT work (simple!):** trained on trillions of words to predict **the next token**, billions of times over; a giant neural net (billions of parameters!) learns grammar, facts, reasoning patterns. Ask a question → it generates one word at a time, each chosen by probability 🎲→📜\n- **How BitBot works (me!):** same idea, tiny scale — a hand-built neural net trained on patterns right in your browser. No internet needed, no data leaves your device — 'local AI' 🔒\n- **AI around you daily:** YouTube recommendations, face unlock, Google Translate, UPI fraud detection, autocorrect, maps ETA, exam OMR checking!\n- **Deepfakes:** AI face/voice fakes — check sources, watermarks coming; India's IT rules target them ⚠️\n- **Jobs:** AI won't replace humans — humans USING AI will replace those who don't. Learn it as a tool 🛠️\n- Ethics: bias, privacy, homework-vs-learning (use AI to UNDERSTAND, not to copy — your quiz has anti-cheat for a reason! 😉)"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "ict_gamedev", patterns: ["how are games made","game engines unity unreal","game development","how to make a video game","html5 javascript games","coding a game","game dev for beginners","arcadecraft games made with what","gaming industry size","game design vs development"],
        responses: ["🎮 **How games are actually made:**\n- **The pipeline:** Idea → Game Design Document (rules, levels, fun-factor!) → Art (sprites, 3D models, animations) → **Code/engine** → Sound & music → Levels → Testing (QA plays it 1000 ways to break it) → Launch → Live updates 🔄\n- **Game engines** (the workbenches — physics, rendering, input all built-in):\n  · **Unity** (C#) — most mobile/indie games, great for 2D & 3D, free to start\n  · **Unreal Engine** (C++/Blueprints) — AAA graphics, Fortnite's engine, stunningly realistic\n  · **Godot** (free, open-source, rising fast! 📈)\n  · **Pygame, Scratch** — perfect FIRST steps for students\n- **Web games — THIS site's playground:** HTML5 **Canvas** + **JavaScript** = games that run in any browser, no install! Your Arcade Hub's classics (Snake, Flappy, Dino, Pac-Man) are exactly this: JS drawing frames 60×/second, keyboard/touch events, collision checks, score state. Three.js powers 3D (like the Car Game 3D!) 🚗\n- **The science of fun:** difficulty curves (flow state — not too easy, not too hard), feedback juice (screenshake, particles, sounds), leaderboards (your site's fastest-time ranking = pure dopamine engineering 😄)\n- **Industry:** gaming is BIGGER than movies+music combined (~$200B+); India's gaming scene is exploding (BGMI, Ludo King — made in India! 🇮🇳)\n- Want to start? Learn JS → build Snake on canvas → you'll understand 80% of game dev. Then try Godot/Unity! 🚀"] });

    /* ══════════ MATH concepts (theory to match the engine) ══════════ */
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_bodmas", patterns: ["bodmas rule","bodmas full form","order of operations","dmvas rule","precedence of operators maths","which operation comes first in maths","bodmas kya hai","vbodmas","simplification rules class 8","operation order brackets of division"],
        responses: ["🧮 **BODMAS — the traffic rules of maths:**\n**B**rackets → **O**f (orders/powers, i.e. ² ³ √) → **D**ivision → **M**ultiplication → **A**ddition → **S**ubtraction\n(You'll also see **VBODMAS** — V for Vinculum, the overline bar — and **PEMDAS** in US books: same law!)\n\n**The fine print pros know:**\n1. Brackets innermost-first: ( ) then [ ] then { }\n2. **D & M are EQUAL rank** → solve **left to right** (same for A & S!)\n3. Powers before multiply: 2×3² = 2×9 = **18**, NOT (2×3)²=36!\n\n**Worked example:** `8 + 2 × (6 − 3)² ÷ 9`\n· Brackets: 6−3 = 3 → `8 + 2 × 3² ÷ 9`\n· Order: 3² = 9 → `8 + 2 × 9 ÷ 9`\n· × and ÷ left-to-right: 2×9 = 18 → 18÷9 = 2 → `8 + 2`\n· = **10** ✅\n\n**Classic trap:** `2 + 2 ÷ 2` — division FIRST = 2+1 = **3** (not 2!). This is literally THE most-asked internet maths question 🌐\n\nType me 'simplify and explain: 2+2÷2' and I'll walk you through it step by step — I have a full BODMAS engine inside me now 😎⚙️"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_squares", patterns: ["perfect squares","square root","how to find square root","square root by division method","squares 1 to 25","what is a square number","square root properties","perfect square examples","varg aur vargmul","square root of a number explained"],
        responses: ["🔢 **Squares & square roots — the power of 2:**\n- **Square (n²):** a number multiplied by itself: 5² = 5×5 = 25. Geometrically: the AREA of a square with side 5! ⬛\n- **Perfect squares:** 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144, 169, 196, 225 (up to 15²) — memorize these, they're everywhere in exams!\n- **Square root (√n):** the reverse — 'which number squared gives n?' √49 = 7 because 7²=49. Every positive number has TWO roots (+7 and −7; √ symbol means the positive one)\n- **Cool properties:**\n  · Squares end only in 0,1,4,5,6,9 → a number ending in 2,3,7,8 is NEVER a perfect square 🕵️\n  · Between n² and (n+1)² there are exactly **2n non-square numbers**\n  · 1+3+5+7+… (first n odd numbers) = n² — that's why 1,4,9,16,25 keep appearing!\n- **Finding roots without a calculator — division method:** pair digits from the right (6|25 for 625), largest square ≤ leftmost pair, bring down pairs, double the quotient for the new divisor… √625 = 25. (Ask me '√625' and I'll just tell you ⚡)\n- Perfect squares are the atoms of the **Pythagorean theorem** (3²+4²=5² → 9+16=25) and quadratics. Ask me 'what is 17²' or '√289' anytime!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_cubes", patterns: ["perfect cubes","cube root","cubes 1 to 15","what is a cube in maths","cube root properties","ghanshala cube","cube numbers examples","how to find cube root","cube of 12","perfect cube list"],
        responses: ["🧊 **Cubes & cube roots — the power of 3:**\n- **Cube (n³):** n × n × n → 3³ = 27. Geometrically: VOLUME of a cube with side 3! 📦\n- **Perfect cubes to memorize:** 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000 (1³→10³), then 1331, 1728, 2197, 2744, 3375 (11³→15³)\n- **Cube root (∛):** reverse — ∛27 = 3. Unlike square roots, **negative numbers have real cube roots**: (−2)³ = −8, so ∛−8 = −2 ✅\n- **Tricks:**\n  · Cube endings: 2³=8 ↔ 8³=512 — the last-digit pairs swap (2↔8, 3↔7)! Instant ∛ of perfect cubes: ∛1728 → ends in 8 → root ends in 2; 1728 is between 10³ and 20³… = **12** 🎩\n  · Sum of first n cubes = (sum of first n numbers)² → 1³+2³+3³ = 36 = 6². Beautiful!\n- **Where cubes rule:** volume problems (tank capacity!), density, the 'cube root by prime factorization' exam favourite: 1728 = 2⁶×3³ → pairs of three → 2²×3 = 12\n- Ask me '12³', 'cube root of 343', or 'cube 1 to 20' — full power at your service 💪"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_lcmhcf", patterns: ["lcm and hcf","how to find lcm","hcf full form","lcm full form","lcm hcf by prime factorization","gcd lcm relation","lcm of 12 and 18","hcf of two numbers","laghuttam samapvartak","lcm hcf class 8"],
        responses: ["🔗 **LCM & HCF — the twin tools of numbers:**\n- **HCF/GCD** (Highest Common Factor): the BIGGEST number dividing both. LCM (Lowest Common Multiple): the SMALLEST number both divide into\n- **Example — 12 & 18:**\n  · Factors of 12: 1,2,3,4,6,12 · of 18: 1,2,3,6,9,18 → common: {1,2,3,6} → **HCF = 6**\n  · Multiples of 12: 12,24,36,48… · of 18: 18,36,54… → **LCM = 36**\n- **Prime factorization method (exam favourite):**\n  12 = 2²×3 · 18 = 2×3²\n  · HCF = lowest powers of COMMON primes = 2¹×3¹ = **6**\n  · LCM = highest powers of ALL primes = 2²×3² = **36**\n- **Division method for HCF:** divide bigger by smaller, then divisor by remainder, repeat till remainder 0 — last divisor = HCF (Euclid's algorithm, 300 BCE — still inside every computer!)\n- **THE golden relation:** **HCF × LCM = product of the two numbers** → 6×36 = 216 = 12×18 ✅ (works for exactly 2 numbers!)\n- **Real life:** HCF = splitting into largest equal groups (tiles, teams, measuring rod) · LCM = when events sync again (bells ringing together, circular-track runners meeting, adding fractions 1/12 + 1/18 → LCD 36!)\n- Ask me 'lcm of 20 30' or 'hcf 84 126' — instant! ⚡"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_rational", patterns: ["rational numbers","what is a rational number","properties of rational numbers","rational vs irrational","is 0 a rational number","rational numbers between two numbers","irrational numbers examples","rational number closure property","parimey sankhya","class 8 rational numbers"],
        responses: ["➗ **Rational numbers (Q) — the fraction family:**\n- **Definition:** any number writable as **p/q** where p,q are integers and **q ≠ 0**. So 5 (=5/1), −3/7, 0 (=0/1 ✅ yes, 0 IS rational!), 0.75 (=3/4), 2.333… (=7/3) are ALL rational\n- **Irrational numbers:** CAN'T be written as p/q — decimals that never end, never repeat: **√2, √3, π, e**. π ≈ 3.14159265… forever without pattern! (22/7 is only a rational approximation 😏)\n- **Together = Real numbers** (the complete number line 📏)\n- **Properties (the exam list):**\n  · **Closure:** +, −, × keep you rational (÷ too, except by 0!)\n  · **Commutative:** a+b = b+a; a×b = b×a (NOT for − and ÷!)\n  · **Associative:** for + and ×\n  · **Additive identity: 0** (a+0=a) · **Multiplicative identity: 1** (a×1=a)\n  · **Additive inverse: −a** (a+(−a)=0) · **Multiplicative inverse: 1/a** (a×1/a=1, a≠0)\n  · **Distributive:** a×(b+c) = ab+ac — the workhorse of algebra!\n- **Infinite density:** between ANY two rationals lie infinite rationals! (Between 1/2 and 1/3? Average = 5/12. Then between those two? Keep going forever 🌀)\n- Finding 'n rationals between a & b': use the LCM trick or keep averaging. Ask me anything like 'is 2.5 rational'!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_linear", patterns: ["linear equations","solve linear equation","linear equations in one variable","transpose method","equations class 8","solve for x","linear equation examples","one variable equation steps","rekhik samikaran","linear equation word problems"],
        responses: ["⚖️ **Linear equations in one variable — algebra's entry ticket:**\n- **What:** an equation where the variable's power is 1, like **2x + 3 = 11** or x/2 + 5 = 3x − 4. 'Linear' = its graph is a straight LINE 📈\n- **The golden rule:** whatever you do to one side, do to the OTHER (an equation is a BALANCE — tip one side, tip both! ⚖️)\n- **Steps (transpose method):**\n  1. Simplify both sides (expand brackets, combine like terms)\n  2. Move variable terms to one side, numbers to the other (transposing flips signs: +3 becomes −3)\n  3. Divide by the coefficient\n\n**Example: 3(x − 2) + 4 = 2x + 5**\n· Expand: 3x − 6 + 4 = 2x + 5 → 3x − 2 = 2x + 5\n· Transpose 2x left, −2 right: 3x − 2x = 5 + 2\n· **x = 7** ✅ Check: LHS = 3(5)+4 = 19, RHS = 14+5 = 19 ✔️\n\n**Word problem classic:** 'The sum of two numbers is 25, one number is 5 more than the other.' → x + (x+5) = 25 → 2x = 20 → **x=10, other=15** 🎯\n**Age problems:** 'Father is 3× son's age; in 10 years he'll be 2×.' → 3x+10 = 2(x+10) → x=10 (son now 10, father 30)\n\nAlways CHECK your answer by plugging it back — the habit of good mathematicians (and good coders!). Send me any equation to solve!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_identities", patterns: ["algebraic identities","a plus b whole square","a2 minus b2 formula","standard identities class 8","expansion of a b c square","algebra formulas list","important identities maths","ab ka formula","identities ka table","cube identity formulas"],
        responses: ["🧬 **Algebraic identities — algebra's cheat codes:**\n1. **(a+b)² = a² + 2ab + b²**\n2. **(a−b)² = a² − 2ab + b²**\n3. **(a+b)(a−b) = a² − b²** ← the difference-of-squares (factors EVERYTHING: 999² − 1² = (1000)(998)!)\n4. **(x+a)(x+b) = x² + (a+b)x + ab**\n5. **(a+b+c)² = a² + b² + c² + 2ab + 2bc + 2ca**\n6. **(a+b)³ = a³ + 3a²b + 3ab² + b³** = a³ + b³ + 3ab(a+b)\n7. **(a−b)³ = a³ − 3a²b + 3ab² − b³**\n8. **a³ + b³ = (a+b)(a² − ab + b²)**\n9. **a³ − b³ = (a−b)(a² + ab + b²)**\n10. **a³ + b³ + c³ − 3abc = (a+b+c)(a² + b² + c² − ab − bc − ca)** — and if a+b+c = 0 then a³+b³+c³ = 3abc (party trick! 🎩)\n\n**Memory hooks:** identity #3 sign pattern for cubes: #8 keeps 'a+b', middle minus · #9 keeps 'a−b', middle plus (opposite vibes).\n\n**Why they matter:** mental-maths speed (103×97 = (100+3)(100−3) = 10000−9 = **9991** — in 2 seconds! ⚡), factoring quadratics later, and they're PROVEN by area diagrams: draw a square of side (a+b), split it → the pieces literally ARE a², ab, ab, b².\n\nAsk me to expand or factorize anything — or type '(a+3)² expand' style questions!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_coordinate", patterns: ["coordinate geometry","cartesian plane","quadrants of coordinate plane","x axis y axis","ordered pair coordinates","graph plotting points","coordinates class 8","origin in graph","cartesian system explained","how to plot points on graph"],
        responses: ["📍 **Coordinate geometry — maths gets a GPS:**\n- **The idea (Descartes, allegedly watching a fly on ceiling tiles 🪰):** describe any point's position with NUMBERS — the **Cartesian plane**\n- **The setup:** horizontal **X-axis**, vertical **Y-axis**, crossing at the **origin (0,0)**. A point = **ordered pair (x, y)**: walk x units sideways, then y units up/down. (3,4) ≠ (4,3) — order matters, like latitude-longitude!\n- **The 4 quadrants (sign map — draw a plus sign, go anticlockwise from top-right):**\n  · Q1: (+,+) · Q2: (−,+) · Q3: (−,−) · Q4: (+,−)\n  · Points ON an axis: (0,5) is on Y-axis, (7,0) on X-axis\n- **Distance formula** (it's just Pythagoras in disguise!): between (x₁,y₁) & (x₂,y₂) → √[(x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²] → (0,0) to (3,4) = √(9+16) = **5**\n- **Midpoint:** ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2) → midpoint of (2,3)&(6,7) = (4,5)\n- **Graphing equations:** y = 2x + 1 → plug x values, get points, join → a LINE (that's why they're 'linear'!). Slope = rise/run tells steepness 📈\n- Used everywhere: maps, game sprites (your Arcade Hub's Snake literally moves on a coordinate grid!), GPS, charts, battleship 😄\n- Ask me to 'plot (3,-4) quadrant?' style questions anytime!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_symmetry", patterns: ["symmetry","line symmetry","rotational symmetry","symmetry in geometry","order of rotational symmetry","mirror symmetry examples","symmetry class 8","axes of symmetry of shapes","asymmetry","symmetry real life examples"],
        responses: ["🦋 **Symmetry — nature's favourite design principle:**\n- **Line (mirror) symmetry:** fold the figure — both halves match perfectly. The fold line = **axis of symmetry** 🪞\n  · Square: 4 axes · Rectangle: 2 · Equilateral triangle: 3 · Circle: INFINITE 🤯 · Isosceles triangle: 1 · Scalene & parallelogram: 0!\n  · Letters: A, H, I, M, O, T, U, V, W, X, Y have vertical/horizontal mirror lines (H & I & O & X have both!)\n- **Rotational symmetry:** rotate the figure about its centre — it looks identical more than once per full turn.\n  · **Order** = how many times it matches in 360°: square = 4 (every 90°), equilateral triangle = 3, rectangle = 2, circle = infinite, pinwheel/fan = depends on blades\n  · Angle of rotation = 360° ÷ order\n- **Where symmetry lives:** your face (roughly!), butterfly wings 🦋, snowflakes (6-fold!), starfish (5-fold), honeycombs, rangoli & kolam patterns (Bihar's own art is a symmetry masterclass!), Taj Mahal (bilateral perfection + the calligraphy that shrinks upward so it LOOKS uniform — optical engineering 🕌), molecules, cricket balls!\n- **Asymmetry** is beautiful too: fiddler crab claws, your heartbeat's electrical map, most leaves' vein patterns\n- Maths behind it: **tessellations** (shapes tiling with no gaps — squares, hexagons; Escher's art!) and **groups** (the algebra of symmetry — college maths, but you've just met its baby form 😄)\n- Next time you see a rangoli or a snowflake photo — count its symmetry order. Instant game!"] });
    INTENTS.push({ tag: "math_sets", patterns: ["sets in maths","what is a set","set notation","union intersection of sets","venn diagram","types of sets","empty set subset","set operations examples","ganit set","sets class 11 basics"],
        responses: ["🎯 **Sets — maths' storage boxes:**\n- **Definition:** a well-defined collection of DISTINCT objects ('elements'). Well-defined = no arguments about membership: 'prime numbers < 10' ✅ is a set; 'best cricket players' ❌ is not (opinion!)\n- **Notation:** A = {2, 3, 5, 7} — listing method · or rule form: {x : x is prime, x < 10}. n(A) = number of elements = 4 here\n- **Types:** empty/null set ∅ = { } (no elements — 'even prime > 2'!) · finite/infinite · **subset** A⊆B (every element of A is in B) · universal set U (the everything-box) · equal sets (same elements, order doesn't matter!)\n- **Operations (with A={1,2,3,4}, B={3,4,5,6}):**\n  · **Union A∪B** = {1,2,3,4,5,6} — 'OR': in A or B or both\n  · **Intersection A∩B** = {3,4} — 'AND': in BOTH\n  · **Difference A−B** = {1,2} — in A but not B\n  · **Complement A′** = everything in U not in A\n- **Venn diagrams:** overlapping circles — intersection = the shared lens 🫧\n- **The exam formula:** n(A∪B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A∩B) → 'In a class, 30 play cricket, 25 football, 12 both — how many play at least one?' = 30+25−12 = **43** 🏏⚽\n- Sets (Cantor, 1870s) are the FOUNDATION of all modern maths — every number system is built from sets. Also: SQL databases, Python (`set()`), and logic all speak set language 💻"] });

